# Working notes: plans, critiques, reviews, orchestration prompts.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A SAFETY PATTERN, NOT TIDINESS. These 59 root-level files are
# untracked but not ignored, so `git add -A` sweeps every one of them into a
# commit on a PUBLIC repo. That has now happened twice, caught both times only
# because someone read the file list before pushing. A near-miss caught twice by
# attention is a hazard, not a track record.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ CONVERTED 2026-08-11 to deny-by-default. The two paragraphs below are the
# ORIGINAL reasoning, kept rather than deleted: they contain a real argument that
# deserves answering in place. The answer follows them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# > Scoped to the root (`/`) and to the naming vocabulary these documents
# > actually use — deliberately NOT `/*.md` with an allowlist. A blanket ignore
# > would silently swallow a future CONTRIBUTING.md or SECURITY.md that someone
# > meant to commit, and a file missing from a commit is harder to notice than a
# > file wrongly present in one. Tracked root docs (README, ARCHITECTURE,
# > CHANGELOG) match none of these patterns.
# >
# > ⚠️ The pattern reduces the blast radius; it does not remove it. A planning
# > doc with novel naming is still untracked-and-unignored. The actual fix is to
# > STAGE EXPLICIT PATHS — never `git add -A` or `git add .` in a repo that
# > carries untracked working notes.
#
# THE ANSWER, and what changed.
#
# 1. The vocabulary did not travel. Rolling this block out to the rest of the
#    suite was measured rather than assumed: against spert-scheduler's 108 real
#    filenames it caught FOUR. This repo writes `NAME-CRITIQUE-v2.md`; Scheduler
#    writes `CRITIQUE_name.md`. A copy-paste rollout would have looked like a fix
#    and fired on almost nothing — so the suite standardised on the allowlist,
#    and leaving this repo on the other design would make it the lone exception
#    for no reason.
#
# 2. "A file missing from a commit is harder to notice" is true in general and
#    backwards for a public repo carrying private methodology. The two errors are
#    not symmetrical, and the missing-file case is not in fact silent:
#    `git add CONTRIBUTING.md` on an ignored path EXITS 1 and prints a hint —
#    only `git add -A` passes over it quietly. A doc wrongly ignored is visible
#    on GitHub and fixed by one commit; a planning doc wrongly committed to a
#    public repo is not recoverable, being in the history, cloneable, indexable.
#
# 3. The concession in the second paragraph is the decisive one, and it is this
#    block's own words: "a planning doc with novel naming is still
#    untracked-and-unignored." An allowlist is complete by construction; a
#    vocabulary is incomplete by construction. The closing advice — stage
#    explicit paths, never `git add -A` — remains exactly right, and is still the
#    only thing here covering a doc written into a SUBDIRECTORY, which `/*.md`
#    (root-scoped) does not reach.
#
# ✅ One charge was investigated and did NOT stick — recorded so nobody re-opens
#    it. Elsewhere in the suite this vocabulary style proved self-flattering on
#    macOS: APFS is case-insensitive, so `/*-PLAN-V*.md` matches
#    `...-plan-v3_1.md` locally and nothing in CI, inflating a measured catch
#    rate from 4 to 16. This block was re-measured against its own 60 planning
#    documents under `git config core.ignorecase false` and scored 60/60 — the
#    same as on macOS. Its filenames genuinely use the shouty case its patterns
#    expect, so there was no hidden deflation here. This conversion is about
#    completeness, not repair.
#
# Derive the allowlist, never recall it:   git ls-files ':(glob)*.md'
/*.md
!/ARCHITECTURE.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/README.md
# Conventional root docs, not present today; listed so adding one needs no edit here.
!/SECURITY.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md

# mutation testing (Stryker) — reports are measurements, not artifacts.
# A crashed run also leaves full copies of mutated source in .stryker-tmp,
# which ESLint would otherwise lint.
reports/mutation/
.stryker-tmp/
